"comparison": "Both Goldman Sachs and JPMorgan Chase conducted annual goodwill impairment tests in Q4 2024 and concluded that no impairment was required because the fair value of each reporting unit exceeded its carrying value. Goldman Sachs determined this via qualitative assessment, stating it was 'more likely than not that the estimated fair value... exceeded its respective carrying value' (gs_2024_7_P032_S010, gs_2024_7_P032_S011). JPMorgan similarly concluded 'goodwill was not impaired as of December 31, 2024,' noting that for each reporting unit, 'fair value exceeded carrying value by at least 10%' (jpm_2024_7_P799_S002, jpm_2024_7_P799_S003). While both found fair value to be above carrying value, JPMorgan quantifies the headroom (at least 10%) whereas Goldman Sachs relies on the qualitative 'more likely than not' threshold without specifying the magnitude of the excess in this specific sentence.",
